# 3. PRIORITY ONE: INVENTORY AND STOCK INTEGRITY

Upgrade the inventory subsystem so that all stock-changing activities are traceable and consistent.

## 3.1 Unified stock movement architecture

Create or strengthen a common stock movement service that supports the existing inventory architecture.

Every applicable movement must record:

* Unique movement ID.
* Movement type.
* Transaction date.
* Product or raw-material ID.
* Quantity.
* Unit of measure.
* Source location and destination location, where applicable.
* Source transaction type and ID.
* Production batch or product lot, where applicable.
* User who performed the action.
* Creation timestamp.
* Status.
* Reversal reference, when applicable.
* Notes or reason for adjustments.

Support the business movement categories required by existing workflows, including:

* Opening balance.
* Purchase receipt.
* Production consumption.
* Milling input and output.
* Production output.
* Bulk-to-packaged conversion.
* Dispatch.
* Customer return.
* Supplier return.
* Approved stock adjustment.
* Reversal.

Use existing movement naming conventions where they are already established.

Do not double-count movements already recorded by existing production or packaging processes.

## 3.2 Stock calculation

Implement or consolidate the appropriate stock calculation rules.

Conceptually:

Closing stock = Opening stock + Receipts + Production output + Accepted returns - Consumption - Dispatch - Supplier returns - Other valid issues ± Approved adjustments.

Apply the correct movement signs for each product or material.

Do not assume that every movement category applies to every stock type.

## 3.3 Raw-material receiving

Implement purchasing-related receipt integration.

A purchase order alone must not increase stock.

An accepted goods receipt must create the appropriate stock movement.

Rejected quantities must remain distinguishable from accepted quantities.

Support partial deliveries and multiple receipts against one purchase order.

Prevent over-receipt where business rules prohibit it, or require authorized handling when over-receipt is allowed.

## 3.4 Raw-material consumption

Connect production consumption to the stock movement architecture.

Prevent duplicate consumption when requests are retried.

Preserve production batch references.

Make failed or incomplete operations recoverable without blindly repeating stock deductions.

## 3.5 Opening stock and reconciliation

Implement safe reconciliation facilities.

Never invent opening stock.

Allow authorized users to record a verified opening balance with supporting information.

Preserve the previous value and an audit trail for corrections.

Where stock cannot be reconciled, record an explicit unresolved discrepancy instead of silently changing the quantity.

